          %h2#Terminology Terminology
          %p The term “∞-groupoid” is sometimes considered to be too unwieldy, and some alternatives have been suggested or used, but none has gained wide acceptance.
          %p
            Historically, the word “space” is often (ab)used to mean “∞-groupoid”, due to the traditional presentation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Gpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty Gpd
            by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} model structure on topological spaces
            %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/4781/against-spaces-in-homotopy-theory/"} condemned
            this usage, but others argue that a “homotopy space” is a valid notion of a “space”.
          %p
            The term “space” is also often used to refer to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield"} Bousfield
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan"} Kan
            in their book “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Limits%2C+Completions+and+Localizations"}> Homotopy Limits, Completions and Localizations
            ” write:
          %blockquote
            %p
              “These notes are written
              = succeed "," do
                %em simplicially
              %em space
              we mean
              = succeed "." do
                %em simplicial set
          %p
            More recently,
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
          %p
            The term “homotopy type” is also quite close in meaning to “∞-groupoid”. Historically, it differed in that morphisms of homotopy types were mere homotopy classes of maps of ∞-groupoids, but, more recently (especially with the advent of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "-groupoid\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p#Anima
            More radically,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dustin+Clausen"} Dustin Clausen
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"} Peter Scholze
            use the term “anima” (plural: anima) as a synonym for “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-groupoid”, in particular in relation to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+mathematics"} condensed mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2020/03/pyknoticity_versus_cohesivenes.html#c057623"} nCafe
            %em Purity for flat cohomology
            by Kęstutis Česnavičius and Peter Scholze they write “If the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are called widgets, then we call those of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ani
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ani(C)
            animated widgets, except that we abbreviate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ani
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ani(Set)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ani
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ani
            and the term ‘animated set’ to anima (plural: anima).”
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , Section 1.1.2 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/titles/8957.html"} pup:8957
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/HTT.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Formulations in/from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benno+van+den+Berg"}> Benno van den Berg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Types are weak omega-groupoids
                %strong 102
                2 (2011) 370-394 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.0298"}> arXiv:0812.0298
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/pdq026"} doi:10.1112/plms/pdq026
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"}> Thorsten Altenkirch
                , Ondrej Rypácek,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    A Syntactical Approach to Weak
                    = succeed "-Groupoids" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi ω
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                %strong 16
                (2012) 16-30 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~txa/publ/weakomega2.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2012.16"} doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2012.16
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} category object in an (infinity,1)-category
            for more along these lines.
